/**
\addtogroup sparse

The `laplace::sparse` module provides a sparse symmetric \f$L D L^\top\f$ factorization, linear solver, selected inverse, and selected-inverse derivative in `<laplace/sparse/sparse_ldlt.hpp>`. Under one public interface, \ref laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt "sparse_ldlt" runs either a `simplicial` route (Eigen's `SimplicialLDLT`) or a `supernodal` route with a dense trailing supernode parallelized with OpenMP. Like \ref dense "the dense module", the Eigen backend (Guennebaud and others, 2010), optional LAPACK kernels, and optional device kernels are compiled into `laplace_sparse` (CMake target `Laplace::sparse`) as standard C++ and never by `nvcc`, so the public header includes no Eigen or LAPACK types. Functions in this module are not marked `LAPLACE_HD`; `simplicial` runs on one host thread per call, while `supernodal` uses OpenMP when available and offloads large trailing dense blocks to the GPU via cuSOLVER and cuBLAS in CUDA builds.

## Matrix format

\ref laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt "sparse_ldlt" factorizes a shifted symmetric \f$n \times n\f$ matrix \f$A + \lambda I\f$, where \f$A\f$ is supplied by its lower triangle (diagonal included) in compressed sparse columns, \f$\lambda \in \mathbb{R}\f$ is an optional scalar shift, and \f$I\f$ is the \f$n \times n\f$ identity matrix:
- `col_ptr` holds \f$n + 1\f$ column offsets into `row_idx` and `values`, with `col_ptr[n]` giving the number of stored lower-triangular entries.
- `row_idx` holds the row index of each stored entry with `row >= column`, sorted in ascending order within each column. When a nonzero shift \f$\lambda\f$ is passed, the diagonal entry of each column must be stored first at `col_ptr[j]`.
- `values` holds the numerical values corresponding to `row_idx`.

## Routes and symbolic analysis

Both routes analyze the sparsity pattern (`n`, `col_ptr`, `row_idx`) on the first call to \ref laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt::factorize "factorize()" using Eigen's approximate minimum degree (AMD) fill-reducing ordering (Amestoy, Davis and Duff, 1996; Guennebaud and others, 2010). Repeated factorizations with the same pattern reuse that symbolic analysis and read only `values`.

The route is controlled by \ref laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt_route "sparse_ldlt_route": `automatic` (the default), `simplicial`, or `supernodal`. Under `automatic`, symbolic analysis inspects the width \f$m\f$ of the trailing dense supernode and chooses `supernodal` when \f$m \ge 64\f$. Across crossed-grouping matrices (\f$G\f$ from \f$5\f$ to \f$1000\f$) and single-grouping matrices (\f$G\f$ from \f$10^2\f$ to \f$10^5\f$) on \f$1\f$ and \f$12\f$ threads with both host dense-kernel realizations, the two routes cost within a factor of \f$0.95\f$ to \f$1.2\f$ at width \f$40\f$. At width \f$80\f$, `supernodal` is \f$1.1\f$ to \f$2.7\f$ times faster, so \f$64\f$ keeps `simplicial` near the crossover.

Calling \ref laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt::set_route "set_route()" sets the requested route and forces the next `factorize()` call to re-analyze the pattern. Calling \ref laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt::route "route()" reports the route selected by the last analysis (`simplicial` or `supernodal`), or `automatic` before the first factorization and after `set_route()` changes the request. Both routes provide the same public member functions.

The outcome of `factorize()` is reported as a \ref laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt_outcome "sparse_ldlt_outcome":
- `positive_definite`: the factorization succeeded and every pivot is finite and strictly positive.
- `not_positive_definite`: Eigen's factorization failed or found a non-positive pivot (`simplicial`), or a pivot in the leading columns was non-positive or the dense Cholesky factorization of the Schur complement failed (`supernodal`).
- `non_finite`: an entry of `values` or a computed pivot is non-finite.

After any outcome other than `positive_definite`, pivots and the log determinant are unavailable (quiet NaN on `supernodal`), while \ref laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt::factor_nonzeros "factor_nonzeros()" remains defined and returns the number of entries of \f$L\f$ strictly below its unit diagonal (\f$0\f$ before the first analysis). After `positive_definite`, \ref laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt::solve "solve()" solves \f$(A + \lambda I) z = b\f$ in place on a host array `b` of length \f$n\f$ (returning `false` if unfactorized). \ref laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt::min_pivot "min_pivot()" and \ref laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt::max_pivot "max_pivot()" return the extreme pivots (\f$0.0\f$ for \f$n = 0\f$), and \ref laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt::log_abs_determinant "log_abs_determinant()" returns \f$\log|\det(A + \lambda I)|\f$ (\f$0.0\f$ for \f$n = 0\f$).

## Simplicial route

On the `simplicial` route, \ref laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt::factorize "factorize()" delegates to Eigen's `SimplicialLDLT` with AMD ordering, computing \f$P (A + \lambda I) P^\top = L D L^\top\f$ on one host thread, where \f$P\f$ is the AMD permutation, \f$L\f$ is unit lower triangular, and \f$D\f$ is diagonal.

## Supernodal route

On the `supernodal` route, symbolic analysis applies the same AMD permutation \f$P\f$, builds the elimination tree (Liu, 1990) and column counts by row subtrees, and identifies the trailing dense supernode \f$T\f$: the last \f$m\f$ columns whose column counts are \f$m, m - 1, \dots, 1\f$. Write \f$B\f$ for the leading \f$n - m\f$ columns and \f$A\f$ for the permuted shifted matrix \f$P (A + \lambda I) P^\top\f$.

An up-looking sparse recursion factorizes \f$B\f$ as \f$L D_B L^\top\f$ with unit lower-triangular \f$L\f$ and diagonal \f$D_B\f$, and computes the \f$T\f$-rows of those columns, \f$L_{TB}\f$. The dense \f$m \times m\f$ Schur complement
\f[
S = A_{TT} - L_{TB} D_B L_{TB}^\top
\f]
is assembled densely in parallel over its columns and factorized as \f$S = R R^\top\f$ by dense Cholesky, where \f$R\f$ is lower triangular. Then
\f[
\log|\det(A + \lambda I)| = \sum_{j \in B} \log D_j + 2 \sum_{t \in T} \log R_{tt},
\f]
with pivots \f$D_j\f$ for \f$j \in B\f$ and \f$R_{tt}^2\f$ for \f$t \in T\f$. Calling \ref laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt::solve "solve()" applies \f$P\f$, solves through \f$L\f$ on \f$B\f$, solves densely with \f$R\f$ on \f$T\f$, scales \f$B\f$ by \f$D_B^{-1}\f$, solves through \f$L^\top\f$ on \f$B\f$, and reverses \f$P\f$. Only \f$T\f$ is treated densely; wide supernodes below \f$T\f$ stay sparse.

## Dense kernels and build configuration

Operations on the dense block \f$S\f$ go through an internal interface compiled into `laplace_sparse`. At CMake configure time, `LAPLACE_DENSE_KERNELS` (`auto`, `lapack`, or `eigen`; default `auto`) selects the host realization:
- The environment's LAPACK and BLAS via standard Fortran symbols (`dpotrf`, `dpotri`, `dpotrs`, and `dsymm`; Anderson and others, 1999), from `LAPLACE_LAPACK_LIBRARIES` or `find_package(LAPACK)`. Because these use 32-bit integers (LP64), an ILP64 library must not be linked.
- Dense kernels on Eigen otherwise.

Under `auto`, CMake picks LAPACK when `LAPLACE_LAPACK_LIBRARIES` is set or `find_package(LAPACK)` succeeds, and Eigen otherwise; the configure log prints the choice. OpenMP is optional: without it loops run serially, and with it loops run on one thread below \f$10^5\f$ factor entries. With OpenMP BLAS, small-block calls run on one thread (`dpotrf` for \f$m < 1024\f$, `dpotri` and `dpotrs` for \f$m < 2048\f$, and `dsymm` for \f$m < 128\f$).

## Dense root block on the device

In a CUDA build, the `supernodal` route runs the trailing dense operations on the GPU through cuSOLVER and cuBLAS: the Cholesky factorization \f$S = R R^\top\f$, the solve with \f$R\f$, the inverse \f$Z_{TT} = S^{-1}\f$ from \f$R\f$, and the symmetric product \f$Q = \dot S Z_{TT}\f$. Both \f$R\f$ and \f$Z_{TT}\f$ stay resident on the device between calls. Each factorization copies \f$S\f$ up to the device and the diagonal of \f$R\f$ back to the host. Each solve copies the right-hand vector of length \f$m\f$ up to the device and the solution back. Each selected inverse copies \f$R\f$ to \f$Z_{TT}\f$ on the device and copies \f$Z_{TT}\f$ back after inversion so Takahashi's recursion on \f$B\f$ can read it on the host. Each derivative direction copies \f$\dot S\f$ up and \f$Q\f$ back for the differentiated recursion on \f$B\f$. All calls run on the current device's default stream from host code outside any parallel region, compiled as standard C++ and never by `nvcc`.

The CMake option `LAPLACE_DENSE_KERNELS_DEVICE` (`auto`, `cuda`, or `OFF`; default `auto`) enables the device block: `auto` compiles it in a CUDA build of Kokkos when cuSOLVER and cuBLAS are found and builds host-only otherwise, `cuda` requires them, and `OFF` omits it. While the `supernodal` route starts at \f$m \ge 64\f$ columns, root blocks with \f$m \ge 256\f$ go to the device and smaller blocks use the host kernels of the same build. On crossed-grouping models (RTX 4070 SUPER, FP64 at \f$1/64\f$ of FP32, \f$12\f$ host threads) against the MSVC Eigen host block, each device call costs about \f$0.12\f$ ms regardless of size; factorization, selected inverse, and one derivative direction break even near width \f$150\f$, and the device is faster from about \f$290\f$.

Creating a device block allocates \f$4 m^2\f$ doubles plus workspaces after checking free device memory with `cudaMemGetInfo`; a block that does not fit or fails allocation runs on the host kernels and increments a fallback counter. Two simultaneous blocks hold \f$8 m^2\f$ doubles (\f$5.9\f$ GB at \f$m = 9585\f$), so on a \f$12\f$ GB card the second falls back from about \f$m = 13500\f$. A non-positive-definite leading minor returns `not_positive_definite` as on the host; any other CUDA, cuSOLVER, or cuBLAS error throws `std::runtime_error`, and a later `factorize()` starts over. The routines called are `cusolverDnXpotrf`, `cusolverDnXpotrs`, `cusolverDnDpotri`, and `cublasDsymm_64`. cuSOLVER and cuBLAS are under NVIDIA's CUDA EULA and are linked dynamically; nothing of them is part of Laplace.

At crossed \f$G = 10^4\f$ on the RTX 4070 SUPER (FP64 at \f$1/64\f$ of FP32, \f$12\f$ host threads), steady-state factorization, selected inverse, and one derivative direction take \f$0.68\f$ s, \f$3.80\f$ s, and \f$5.29\f$ s on the device against \f$26.8\f$ s, \f$42.3\f$ s, and \f$85.2\f$ s on the host kernels of the same build. The gcc build with OpenBLAS takes \f$0.72\f$ s, \f$3.78\f$ s, and \f$4.04\f$ s, so on this card the device costs about what the host costs with an optimized BLAS.

## Selected inverse

Write \f$Z = (A + \lambda I)^{-1}\f$. Calling \ref laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt::compute_selected_inverse "compute_selected_inverse()" computes \f$Z\f$ on the sparsity pattern of \f$L + L^\top\f$ (fill included). On `simplicial`, Takahashi's recursion (Takahashi, Fagan and Chin, 1973; Erisman and Tinney, 1975) sweeps over columns \f$j = n - 1, \dots, 0\f$ in the permuted ordering:
\f[
Z_{ij} = -\sum_{k \in \mathcal{J}_j} Z_{ik} L_{kj} \quad (i \in \mathcal{J}_j), \qquad
Z_{jj} = \frac{1}{D_j} - \sum_{k \in \mathcal{J}_j} L_{kj} Z_{kj},
\f]
where \f$\mathcal{J}_j\f$ is the set of row indices below the diagonal in column \f$j\f$ of \f$L\f$. Every \f$Z_{ik}\f$ read lies in the pattern because rows in \f$\mathcal{J}_j\f$ are pairwise joined in \f$L\f$. The recursion costs about one numeric factorization and stores one `double` per subdiagonal entry of \f$L\f$ plus \f$n\f$ diagonal entries.

On `supernodal`, \f$Z_{TT} = S^{-1}\f$ is formed from \f$R\f$, and Takahashi's recursion runs over \f$B\f$ in parallel across independent subtrees below \f$T\f$.

`compute_selected_inverse()` returns `false` if unfactorized, and a later `factorize()` discards it. \ref laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt::selected_inverse_entry "selected_inverse_entry(i, j)" takes original indices in either order and throws `std::out_of_range` outside \f$L + L^\top\f$ or when uncomputed. In `tests/sparse/test_selected_inverse.cpp`, every pattern entry is compared against `laplace::dense::spd_solve()` on six matrices (\f$1 \times 1\f$, tridiagonal, arrowhead, random sparse at shifts \f$0\f$ and \f$0.75\f$, and crossed groupings); the largest error is \f$8.0 \times 10^{-16} \max_{i,j} |Z_{ij}|\f$ on gcc and \f$6.0 \times 10^{-16} \max_{i,j} |Z_{ij}|\f$ on CUDA (gcc and MSVC hosts), within \f$10^{-13} \max_{i,j} |Z_{ij}|\f$.

## Derivative of the selected inverse

Calling \ref laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt::compute_selected_inverse_derivative "compute_selected_inverse_derivative(dvalues)" computes \f$\dot Z = -Z \dot A Z\f$ on the pattern of \f$L + L^\top\f$, where \f$\dot A\f$ is symmetric with lower-triangular `dvalues` aligned with `col_ptr` and `row_idx`. On `simplicial`, a left-looking sweep in the permuted ordering gives \f$\dot L\f$ and \f$\dot D\f$ on the pattern of \f$L\f$ (\f$L_{jj} = 1\f$, \f$\dot L_{jj} = 0\f$):
\f[
\dot L_{ij} D_j + L_{ij} \dot D_j = \dot A_{ij} - \sum_{k < j} \left(\dot L_{ik} L_{jk} D_k + L_{ik} \dot L_{jk} D_k + L_{ik} L_{jk} \dot D_k\right), \qquad i \ge j.
\f]
Differentiating Takahashi's recursion over \f$j = n - 1, \dots, 0\f$ yields \f$\dot Z\f$ on \f$L + L^\top\f$:
\f[
\dot Z_{ij} = -\sum_{k \in \mathcal{J}_j} \left(\dot Z_{ik} L_{kj} + Z_{ik} \dot L_{kj}\right) \quad (i \in \mathcal{J}_j), \qquad
\dot Z_{jj} = -\frac{\dot D_j}{D_j^2} - \sum_{k \in \mathcal{J}_j} \left(\dot L_{kj} Z_{kj} + L_{kj} \dot Z_{kj}\right).
\f]
Each direction costs \f$0.65\f$ to \f$1.1\f$ times the sum of a numeric factorization and selected inverse, using three arrays of the size of \f$L\f$.

On `supernodal`, the derivative proceeds in four steps:
1. The up-looking recursion is differentiated on \f$B\f$ and the \f$B\f$-columns of \f$T\f$. With \f$y_i = L_{ki} D_i\f$, its derivative \f$\dot y_i\f$ follows the same triangular recursion, giving
   \f[
   \dot L_{ki} = \frac{\dot y_i - L_{ki} \dot D_i}{D_i}, \qquad
   \dot D_k = \dot A_{kk} - \sum_{i} \left(\dot L_{ki} y_i + L_{ki} \dot y_i\right).
   \f]
2. The dense Schur-complement derivative is formed as
   \f[
   \dot S = \dot A_{TT} - \left(\dot L_{TB} D_B L_{TB}^\top + L_{TB} \dot D_B L_{TB}^\top + L_{TB} D_B \dot L_{TB}^\top\right).
   \f]
3. One dense symmetric product computes \f$Q = \dot S Z_{TT}\f$, so \f$\dot Z_{TT} = -Z_{TT} Q\f$. Entries \f$\dot Z_{ab} = -Z_{TT}(:, a)^\top Q(:, b)\f$ for \f$a, b \in T\f$ are read where needed.
4. Differentiated Takahashi recursion runs in parallel over subtrees below \f$T\f$. For a column \f$j \in B\f$ with \f$c\f$ rows in \f$T\f$, pairs with both rows in \f$T\f$ use an intermediate vector \f$u\f$ of length \f$m\f$,
   \f[
   \sum_{k \in \mathcal{J}_j \cap T} \dot Z_{ik} L_{kj} = -Z_{TT}(:, i)^\top u, \qquad
   u = \sum_{k \in \mathcal{J}_j \cap T} Q(:, k) L_{kj},
   \f]
   evaluated in parallel across groups of consecutive \f$B\f$-columns with the same \f$T\f$-rows so each column of \f$Q\f$ and \f$Z_{TT}\f$ is read once per group, costing \f$O(c\, m)\f$ operations instead of \f$O(c^2 m)\f$.

`compute_selected_inverse_derivative()` returns `false` unless `compute_selected_inverse()` ran since the last factorization; a new `factorize()` discards it and another call replaces it. \ref laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt::selected_inverse_derivative_entry "selected_inverse_derivative_entry(i, j)" takes original indices in either order and throws `std::out_of_range` outside the pattern or when uncomputed. \ref laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt::selected_inverse_derivative_dot "selected_inverse_derivative_dot(values)" takes a symmetric \f$V\f$ aligned like `dvalues`, returns \f$s = \sum_{i,j} \dot Z_{ij} V_{ij} = -\operatorname{tr}(Z \dot A Z V)\f$ (counting subdiagonal entries twice), and throws `std::out_of_range` when uncomputed. On the six test matrices with random \f$\dot A\f$ and \f$V\f$, errors against dense \f$-Z \dot A Z\f$ and \f$-\operatorname{tr}(Z \dot A Z V)\f$ are at most \f$1.5 \times 10^{-15} \max_{i,j} |\dot Z_{ij}|\f$ and \f$8 \times 10^{-15}\f$ on gcc and CUDA, within bounds \f$10^{-12} \max_{i,j} |\dot Z_{ij}|\f$ and \f$10^{-11}(1 + |s|)\f$.

## Accuracy

In addition to `tests/sparse/test_selected_inverse.cpp`, `tests/sparse/test_supernodal_ldlt.cpp` compares `supernodal` against `simplicial` on \f$13\f$ matrices: a \f$1 \times 1\f$ matrix, tridiagonal (\f$n = 60\f$), dense (\f$n = 40\f$, all in \f$T\f$), a two-block forest (\f$n = 30\f$), random sparse (\f$n = 200\f$, shifts \f$0\f$ and \f$0.75\f$), dense roots of \f$63\f$, \f$64\f$, \f$255\f$, and \f$256\f$ columns, and three crossed groupings (\f$60 \times 50\f$, \f$150 \times 150\f$, and \f$400 \times 300\f$ levels, up to \f$n = 1102\f$). It also checks refactorization on the same pattern and negative pivots in \f$B\f$ or \f$T\f$. During comparisons, the device threshold is lowered to one column so every root runs on the device in a CUDA build; at the default threshold, the test verifies that \f$255\f$ columns stay on the host and \f$256\f$ use the device, and verifies the fallback under a one-byte cap. The table reports each largest error over its bound's scale (\f$1 + |\log|\det||\f$, \f$\max_i |x_i|\f$, \f$\max_{i,j} |Z_{ij}|\f$, \f$\max_{i,j} |\dot Z_{ij}|\f$, \f$1 + |s|\f$; pivots are relative; see `docs/numerics.md`). The \f$255\f$- and \f$256\f$-column dense roots give the largest solve, \f$Z\f$, and \f$\dot Z\f$ errors.

| Quantity | gcc, LAPACK | gcc, Eigen kernels | CUDA build, device block | Test bound |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| \f$\log|\det|\f$ | \f$1.0 \times 10^{-15}\f$ | \f$1.0 \times 10^{-15}\f$ | \f$1.0 \times 10^{-15}\f$ | \f$10^{-14}\f$ |
| Pivots | \f$4.4 \times 10^{-16}\f$ | \f$4.4 \times 10^{-16}\f$ | \f$6.7 \times 10^{-16}\f$ | \f$2 \times 10^{-15}\f$ |
| Solve | \f$2.0 \times 10^{-15}\f$ | \f$2.0 \times 10^{-15}\f$ | \f$2.0 \times 10^{-15}\f$ | \f$2 \times 10^{-14}\f$ |
| \f$Z\f$ on the pattern | \f$2.4 \times 10^{-15}\f$ | \f$3.2 \times 10^{-15}\f$ | \f$3.3 \times 10^{-15}\f$ | \f$3 \times 10^{-14}\f$ |
| \f$\dot Z\f$ on the pattern | \f$4.7 \times 10^{-15}\f$ | \f$7.0 \times 10^{-15}\f$ | \f$7.0 \times 10^{-15}\f$ | \f$7 \times 10^{-14}\f$ |
| Contraction | \f$3.1 \times 10^{-15}\f$ | \f$3.1 \times 10^{-15}\f$ | \f$1.9 \times 10^{-15}\f$ | \f$5 \times 10^{-14}\f$ |

## Speed

Measured on gcc with OpenBLAS (\f$12\f$ threads unless noted) on crossed-grouping matrices from `benchmarks/engine_scale.cpp`, times in seconds compare the `simplicial` and `supernodal` routes:

| \f$G\f$ | \f$m\f$ | Factorization, simplicial | Factorization, supernodal | Selected inverse, simplicial | Selected inverse, supernodal | Derivative direction, simplicial | Derivative direction, supernodal |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| \f$10^3\f$ | \f$960\f$ | 0.062 s | 0.009 s (1 thread) | 0.34 s | 0.022 s (4 threads) | 0.43 s | 0.016 s |
| \f$3 \times 10^3\f$ | \f$2878\f$ | 2.3 s | 0.052 s | 9.0 s | 0.16 s (4 threads) | 12.5 s | 0.21 s |
| \f$10^4\f$ | \f$9585\f$ | 105 s | 0.79 s | 333 s | 4.2 s | 445 s | 4.0 s |

## Example

The following host snippet factorizes a \f$3 \times 3\f$ tridiagonal symmetric positive-definite matrix stored in lower compressed sparse column format and solves \f$A z = b\f$ in place:

```cpp
#include <laplace/sparse/sparse_ldlt.hpp>

#include <array>

bool solve_tridiagonal_example() {
  const int n = 3;
  const std::array<int, 4> col_ptr = {0, 2, 4, 5};
  const std::array<int, 5> row_idx = {0, 1, 1, 2, 2};
  const std::array<double, 5> values = {2.0, -1.0, 2.0, -1.0, 2.0};

  laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt ldlt;
  if (ldlt.factorize(n, col_ptr.data(), row_idx.data(), values.data()) !=
      laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt_outcome::positive_definite) {
    return false;
  }

  std::array<double, 3> b = {1.0, 0.0, 1.0};
  return ldlt.solve(b.data());  // b now holds {1.0, 1.0, 1.0}
}
```

## Module types

In namespace `laplace::sparse`:
- \ref laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt_outcome "sparse_ldlt_outcome"
- \ref laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt_route "sparse_ldlt_route"
- \ref laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt "sparse_ldlt" (including \ref laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt::set_route "set_route()", \ref laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt::route "route()", \ref laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt::factorize "factorize()", \ref laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt::solve "solve()", \ref laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt::min_pivot "min_pivot()", \ref laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt::max_pivot "max_pivot()", \ref laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt::log_abs_determinant "log_abs_determinant()", \ref laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt::factor_nonzeros "factor_nonzeros()", \ref laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt::compute_selected_inverse "compute_selected_inverse()", \ref laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt::selected_inverse_entry "selected_inverse_entry()", \ref laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt::compute_selected_inverse_derivative "compute_selected_inverse_derivative()", \ref laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt::selected_inverse_derivative_entry "selected_inverse_derivative_entry()", and \ref laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt::selected_inverse_derivative_dot "selected_inverse_derivative_dot()")

